Established 1852.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.573
Re-established 2018.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.560
- [Narrator] Once a bountiful fish

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.630
found in the deep and rocky waters

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.710
along the U.S. South Atlantic
coast and the Gulf of Mexico,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.360
overfishing now poses a serious threat

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.960
to their ability to reproduce.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.812
This is the snowy grouper.

00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:26.395
(gentle music)

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.730
The snowy grouper is
named for the white spots

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.150
that speckle its scales.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.990
As the groupers grow,
these spots tend to blend

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.153
into the fish's natural brown color.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.570
They will often eat crabs and small squid

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.103
that they swallow whole
with their large mouths.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.760
Snowy groupers all begin life as females,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.090
but as they age, some become males.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.090
Since the 1990s, overfishing of juveniles

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.900
has affected populations,

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.710
because not enough fish
reach maturity to change sex.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.630
Regulations were put in
place, but not enough was done

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.440
to slow the overfishing of the species.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.290
The snowy grouper is now
listed as vulnerable,

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.093
and its populations continue to decline.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.600
This is the snowy grouper.

